        "q1": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "When can a baby start using a sleep bag?",
            "answer": "<p>From birth. Our sleep bags start at 0–3M (3–6kg). Ensure the neck opening is snug enough that baby can't slip inside — check the size guide.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Is TENCEL™ safe for newborns?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. TENCEL™ Lyocell is naturally hypoallergenic and listed by the National Eczema Society as suitable for sensitive skin. All our fabrics are OEKO-TEX® certified.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Can my baby wear a sleeper under the sleep bag?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes — most parents do. A fitted TENCEL™ sleeper under a 1.0 TOG bag is the most common combo. Avoid too many layers: one more layer than an adult would wear is a good rule of thumb.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What's the difference between TENCEL, Muslin, and Waffle Knit?",
            "answer": "<p>TENCEL™ Modal is the softest and most temperature-regulating — great year-round. Bamboo Muslin is ultra-lightweight and breathable, ideal for warm weather (0.5 TOG). Waffle Knit provides gentle insulation with built-in airflow — perfect for the 2.5 TOG winter option.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q5": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Are sleep bags good for babies with eczema?",
            "answer": "<p>Many parents tell us yes. TENCEL's smooth fibre surface creates less friction against skin, and there are no flame-retardant chemicals on any of our fabrics. Talk to your paediatrician for your child's specific situation.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q6": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Is TENCEL more sustainable than cotton?",
            "answer": "<p>TENCEL is made in a closed-loop process that recycles roughly 99% of the solvents used. The wood pulp comes from sustainably managed forests. It uses significantly less water than conventional cotton and avoids pesticide use.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
